{"authored" [{"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions does the continuous-partial-derivative sufficient theorem apply throughout the stated parameter domain? Apply the theorem locally on a rectangle $[0,1]\\times[c,d]$: the integrand and its partial derivative with respect to $t$ must both be continuous there, and the partial derivative must exist at every point of the rectangle.\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/review.json" "file_sha256" "4963536a2779d29c9b4154817247ad552ae61087f3a4231551cbc37ff40cb361" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6eea52fe03b8f70fbe91e5c1a487da968c282204dc20a36ba965bf10c783d7f8" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/verification.json" "basis" 2693 "rationale" "The authentic worked solution identifies the continuous-partial sufficient theorem and accepts I/III, but incorrectly treats failure of sufficient conditions as invalidity of interchange. State the intended theorem criterion explicitly, retain the accepted key and choice identities, and explain the valid almost-everywhere dominated-convergence interchange for II. Raw prompts, widgets, grades, history, and original source content remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The stated sufficient theorem applies to I and III. For I, the partial derivative is $-2txe^{-t^2x}$; for III, it is $-2t/(x^2+t^2)^2$. Both integrands and their partial derivatives are continuous on a closed rectangle around every allowed parameter value, taking $c>0$ for III.\n\nFor II, $\\partial_t|x-t|$ equals $-1$ when $x>t$ and $1$ when $x<t$, but does not exist at $x=t$. Thus the stated theorem does not apply throughout its parameter domain. Its failure does not prove that interchange is invalid: the difference quotients are bounded in absolute value by $1$ and converge for almost every $x$, so dominated convergence justifies differentiation under the integral for II. Explicitly, $F_2(t)=t^2-t+1/2$ for $0\\le t\\le1$ and $F_2(t)=t-1/2$ for $t\\ge1$, giving $F_2′(t)=2t-1$ on $(0,1)$ and $1$ for $t\\ge1$, with right derivative $-1$ at $0$.\n\nUnder the explicit sufficient-theorem request, the answer is I and III only." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/review.json" "file_sha256" "4963536a2779d29c9b4154817247ad552ae61087f3a4231551cbc37ff40cb361" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6eea52fe03b8f70fbe91e5c1a487da968c282204dc20a36ba965bf10c783d7f8" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/verification.json" "basis" 2693 "rationale" "The authentic worked solution identifies the continuous-partial sufficient theorem and accepts I/III, but incorrectly treats failure of sufficient conditions as invalidity of interchange. State the intended theorem criterion explicitly, retain the accepted key and choice identities, and explain the valid almost-everywhere dominated-convergence interchange for II. Raw prompts, widgets, grades, history, and original source content remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/review.json" "file_sha256" "4963536a2779d29c9b4154817247ad552ae61087f3a4231551cbc37ff40cb361" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6eea52fe03b8f70fbe91e5c1a487da968c282204dc20a36ba965bf10c783d7f8" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335252/verification.json" "basis" 2693 "rationale" "The authentic worked solution identifies the continuous-partial sufficient theorem and accepts I/III, but incorrectly treats failure of sufficient conditions as invalidity of interchange. State the intended theorem criterion explicitly, retain the accepted key and choice identities, and explain the valid almost-everywhere dominated-convergence interchange for II. Raw prompts, widgets, grades, history, and original source content remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa159b8b2c2e0a63b17acc0ba599ae23e7a7523b9966d106ff2f86401129fcd" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b06589f536a568a60fc1910670924fbac977e5ba5ad6e964bdb58232c0b32b8" "basis" 2600 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_c